{:edb/db-before-t 2636 :edb/db-after-t 2637 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535949 50 #inst "2026-10-09T11:20:14.751Z" 13194139535949 true] [17592186157377 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376017 13194139535949 true] [17592186157377 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376024 13194139535949 true] [17592186157377 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376031 13194139535949 true] [17592186157378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376003 13194139535949 true] [17592186157378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376010 13194139535949 true] [17592186376003 1017 #edb/ref 17592186376004 13194139535949 true] [17592186376003 1089 "q-326239" 13194139535949 true] [17592186376003 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535949 true] [17592186376003 1100 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535949 true] [17592186376003 1113 #uuid "b306c145-fe13-5cb2-beb3-85a5684d2dfb" 13194139535949 true] [17592186376003 1145 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5-5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\\,t{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535949 true] [17592186376004 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376005 13194139535949 true] [17592186376004 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376006 13194139535949 true] [17592186376004 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376007 13194139535949 true] [17592186376